::ALG2_U03_L03_GP01::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Solve √(x + 6) = 5.
{
= x = 19#Correct. Squaring gives x + 6 = 25, so x = 19, and the original equation checks.
~ x = -1#This subtracts incorrectly after squaring.
~ x = 11#This uses 5 + 6 instead of 25 - 6.
~ x = 31#This adds 6 after squaring instead of subtracting it.
}

::ALG2_U03_L03_GP02::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Solve √(3x + 1) = 4.
{
~ x = 3#This does not make 3x + 1 equal 16.
= x = 5#Correct. Squaring gives 3x + 1 = 16, so 3x = 15 and x = 5.
~ x = 15#This misses the division by 3.
~ x = 17#This adds instead of solving 3x + 1 = 16.
}

::ALG2_U03_L03_GP03::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Which value checks for √(x + 9) = x - 3?
{
~ x = 0 only#x = 0 is extraneous because the right side is -3.
~ x = 0 and x = 7#Both appear after squaring, but x = 0 fails the original equation.
= x = 7 only#Correct. Squaring gives x = 0 or x = 7, but x = 0 gives 3 = -3, so only x = 7 checks.
~ no solution#x = 7 satisfies the original equation.
}

::ALG2_U03_L03_GP04::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
A model d = √(25t) gives distance d. If d = 15, what is t?
{
~ t = 3#This divides 15 by 5 but does not square first.
~ t = 15#This does not solve 225 = 25t.
~ t = 225#This is d squared, not the value of t.
= t = 9#Correct. 15 = √(25t), so 225 = 25t and t = 9.
}

::ALG2_U03_L03_GP05::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L03_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3
Question:
Why must radical equation solutions be checked?
{
= squaring can create extraneous solutions#Correct. Squaring both sides can make a false original equation appear true in the squared equation.
~ radicals cannot have solutions#Radical equations can have valid solutions.
~ checking changes the equation#Checking substitutes into the original equation; it does not change it.
~ all squared equations have no solutions#Squared equations often have solutions, but they must be verified.
}

